text:
Dust never came in my room nor fell from the sky;
I never was troubled by it or had need of a broom.
The sun came up like thunder on the ground at night,
And the moon with her golden feet swept across the skies;
With her face of silver she looked down to see
If I’d been as good as I knew I should be.

The following lines are from the poetry of William Wordsworth;

I wandered lonely as a cloud
That floats on high o'er vales and hills,
When all at once I saw a crowd,
A host, of golden daffodils;
